{"text": "Begin before any print on this wall. At the Heian court by the 12th century, the kosode was underwear, worn next to the skin. As court dress grew simpler, the robes above it came off and the kosode came to the surface. By the early Edo period, its shape was not far from today's kimono. Fashion moved fast. A pattern book printed in 1666 records the new kanbun style, a wide empty back with large moving motifs. Meiji changed the words. Many garment names collapsed into one, kimono. Becky argues that one shape was frozen in time in these years. Magazines took up the teaching. In one, the tsukesage had nearly vanished by the 2000s, folded into the visiting kimono. In 1981, the kimono retail market was worth nearly 1.8 trillion yen. By 2020, it was about a seventh of that. Kimono became something rented rather than owned. By 2021, Kyoto alone had about 40 rental shops. By then, the shape had long since traveled. The wall goes on abroad.", "duration": 75.04, "timestamps": {"word": [{"word": "Begin", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.5}, {"word": "before", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.9}, {"word": "any", "start": 0.98, "end": 1.16}, {"word": "print", "start": 1.3, "end": 1.62}, {"word": "on", "start": 1.68, "end": 1.76}, {"word": "this", "start": 1.82, "end": 2.02}, {"word": "wall.", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.4}, {"word": "At", "start": 3.04, "end": 3.14}, {"word": "the", "start": 3.18, "end": 3.28}, {"word": "Heian", "start": 3.36, "end": 3.74}, {"word": "court", "start": 3.86, "end": 4.22}, {"word": "by", "start": 4.26, "end": 4.38}, {"word": "the", "start": 4.44, "end": 4.52}, {"word": "12th", "start": 4.56, "end": 4.86}, {"word": "century,", "start": 4.94, "end": 5.38}, {"word": "the", "start": 5.74, "end": 5.82}, {"word": "kosode", "start": 5.88, "end": 6.4}, {"word": "was", "start": 6.46, "end": 6.66}, {"word": "underwear,", "start": 6.76, "end": 7.28}, {"word": "worn", "start": 7.66, "end": 7.86}, {"word": "next", "start": 7.92, "end": 8.119}, {"word": "to", "start": 8.18, "